WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.120
A drug stops the cells making any new protein, and interferon still switches genes

2
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.240
on. So the factor must already be in the cell.

3
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.360
Two laboratories went looking, from opposite ends.

4
00:00:19.183 --> 00:00:22.603
James Darnell's laboratory started at the gene.

5
00:00:23.263 --> 00:00:30.443
It found a protein complex, ISGF3, that binds a short DNA sequence shared by

6
00:00:30.443 --> 00:00:32.163
interferon-responsive genes.

7
00:00:33.323 --> 00:00:39.423
In August 1992, Christian Schindler, Ke Shuai and colleagues reported that

8
00:00:39.423 --> 00:00:42.863
interferon puts phosphate on its proteins' tyrosines.

9
00:00:44.363 --> 00:00:48.563
Staurosporine, a drug that blocks many kinases, stopped the activation.

10
00:00:49.523 --> 00:00:51.263
Some kinase had to act first.

11
00:00:52.083 --> 00:00:53.503
Which one, it could not say.

12
00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:59.177
George Stark and Ian Kerr's laboratory started with genetics.

13
00:01:00.177 --> 00:01:05.257
Sandra Pellegrini built human cells with a marker gene controlled by

14
00:01:05.257 --> 00:01:09.917
interferon-responsive DNA. Only cells deaf to interferon survived.

15
00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:14.017
She screened about 100 million mutagenized cells.

16
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:16.937
Each mutant had lost a part of the pathway.

17
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:22.557
She put DNA from normal cells into a mutant, and looked for the rare cell that

18
00:01:22.557 --> 00:01:28.797
recovered. In July 1992, her group in Paris reported the gene that rescued one

19
00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:30.857
mutant: TYK2.

20
00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:34.837
Two weeks apart, the results fit together.

21
00:01:35.457 --> 00:01:40.897
A JAK is required, and the DNA-binding proteins carry phosphotyrosine.

22
00:01:41.737 --> 00:01:44.697
Darnell's laboratory named those proteins STATs.
